 Pass~5 assigns state: each register and memory location the graph names is tied to one physical register or slot of one memory array and reserved, each register operand the graph leaves unspecified ($?$ in Fig.~\ref{fig:relaxation}) gets a register from a free pool, each unspecified memory operand gets a fresh slot that aliases nothing, and every address is an offset from one base pointer, so no access faults. Pass~6 emits LLVM IR~\cite{lattner2004llvm} in which each non-branch instruction is an inline-assembly block with a global PC label, and applies value-neutral additions that change no value the graph specifies: ALU operations spliced into the transmitter's data path and elsewhere, and one to three filler instructions drawn uniformly from 69 x86 instructions (e.g., integer and SSE arithmetic, shifts, bit operations, conditional moves, \texttt{nop}s, and a direct \texttt{jmp} to the next instruction). It emits each program in two versions that differ only in where loaded values are held: the integer version keeps every load result in its integer register, and the vector version moves each load result that a later instruction reads through a randomly chosen vector register (\texttt{xmm0}--\texttt{xmm15}) before that use, because an x86 program can hold a loaded value in either register class, and a defense must protect it in both; the specification does not fix which register holds it, so we draw one at random. The two versions share every other instruction, memory access, branch direction, and timing draw. The taint-tracking defenses track taint per register, so we run both versions on them; the invisible-speculation defenses' transmission criteria do not depend on where a value is held, so we run only the vector version on them. Pass~7 compiles the IR with Clang~15 at \texttt{-O2}, reads each PC label's address back from the binary, and writes the annotation that the executor reads. The compiler cannot drop, reorder, or re-register an instruction the graph specifies: each non-branch one is an \texttt{asm sideeffect} block, which LLVM must assume has side effects it cannot see~\cite{llvmlangref}, and each register the graph fixes is pinned by its register constraint.
 
